    kv(r'generator', r'tools/icls2nvim.py (Python, standard library only)'),
    kv(r'readers', r'lua/island/init.lua, lua/lualine/themes/island-dark.lua'),
    kv(r'tested by', r'tests/theme-island_spec.lua, describe palette.lua'),
    ...sec(r'why this file exists'),
    ...para('--', r'A colour scheme is the kind of file that is easy to start and hard to keep right: hundreds of hex values, each of which is somebody’s decision. xynovim’s theme did not start from taste at a blank page. It started from a JetBrains scheme (the header says scheme "Dark", parent Darcula, from RustRover 2026.2.3.0.0), and this file is that scheme, resolved and flattened into a Lua table that Neovim can read without parsing any XML.'),
    ...para('--', r'The header of the file says exactly what it is and what not to do with it.'),
    ...code('lua', r'lua/island/palette.lua · lines 1-7', r'''
-- GENERATED by tools/icls2nvim.py from Dark.icls. DO NOT EDIT BY HAND;
-- regenerate with: python3 tools/icls2nvim.py YOUR_EXPORT.icls (the original export is not distributed)
-- scheme "Dark" (parent Darcula) from RustRover 2026.2.3.0.0
return {
  name = "Dark",
  colors = {
    ADDED_LINES_COLOR = "#549159",'''),
    ...para('--', r'Three facts are in those lines. The file is generated, so a hand edit would be lost the next time the converter runs. The input is an .icls file, the XML format JetBrains IDEs use to export a colour scheme. And the original export is not distributed. The second line of the header was rewritten on 2026-10-22 for that reason (commit 0da94f4): before, it said to regenerate with a bare python3 tools/icls2nvim.py, which worked because the export, tools/Dark.icls, was in the repository. That commit removed the 983 line export and changed the instruction to pass your own file. The palette is now the only copy of the scheme the repository holds.'),
    ...sec(r'the shape of the table'),
    ...para('--', r'The returned table has four fields: name, colors, derived and attrs. Two of them are plain maps of name to hex string; the third is a map of name to a small record.'),
    blank,
    ...pt('--', r'name', r'the scheme name as the export spells it, "Dark". The spec asserts this literally.'),
    ...pt('--', r'colors', r'the <colors> block of the .icls file, 63 entries: the editor’s single purpose colours such as CARET_ROW_COLOR, LOOKUP_COLOR (popup background), INDENT_GUIDE, RIGHT_MARGIN_COLOR, the FILESTATUS_* family, and the VCS annotation blues. Keys that are not valid Lua identifiers, such as NEXT_EDIT.DIFF_AFTER_BACKGROUND, are written as bracketed strings.'),
    ...pt('--', r'derived', r'three colours the scheme does not define itself but that are taken from other entries of it: BORDER, SELECTION_BACKGROUND and SELECTION_STRONG.'),
    ...pt('--', r'attrs', r'the text attributes, 173 entries, each with any of fg, bg, bold, italic, effect, effect_color and stripe.'),
    ...code('lua', r'lua/island/palette.lua · lines 68-76', r'''
    WHITESPACES = "#6f737a",
    WHITESPACES_MODIFIED_LINES_COLOR = "#52433d",
  },
  derived = {
    BORDER = "#666870",
    SELECTION_BACKGROUND = "#2d416b",
    SELECTION_STRONG = "#2f5194",
  },
  attrs = {'''),
    ...para('--', r'The spec pins the derived colours with exact values (#666870, #2d416b, #2f5194) and also asserts something more telling: that the scheme itself has no SELECTION_BACKGROUND in colors. The converter takes such colours from other entries of the scheme and puts them in derived, and init.lua reads them from D rather than C. Seeing a value in D rather than C is therefore a signal: this one was inferred, not read directly.'),
    ...sec(r'reading an attribute'),
    ...code('lua', r'lua/island/palette.lua · lines 122-126', r'''
    DEFAULT_KEYWORD = { fg = "#cf8e6d" },
    DEFAULT_LINE_COMMENT = { fg = "#7a7e85" },
    DEFAULT_METADATA = { fg = "#b3ae60" },
    DEFAULT_NUMBER = { fg = "#2aacb8" },
    DEFAULT_OPERATION_SIGN = { fg = "#bcbec4" },'''),
    ...para('--', r'The DEFAULT_* entries are JetBrains’ language independent text attributes: keyword, line comment, metadata (annotations and attributes), number, operation sign. They are the backbone of the Neovim theme. The orange keyword #cf8e6d, the grey comment #7a7e85, the teal number #2aacb8 and the grey-white text #bcbec4 are the colours you see in every file of any language.'),
    ...code('lua', r'lua/island/palette.lua · lines 229-249', r'''
    ["org.rust.CFG_DISABLED_CODE"] = { fg = "#8a8e57" },
    ["org.rust.ENUM"] = { fg = "#a6bb77", italic = true },
    ["org.rust.ENUM_VARIANT"] = { fg = "#8cc8d4", italic = true },
    ["org.rust.FUNCTION"] = { fg = "#6aa2d7" },
    ["org.rust.FUNCTION_CALL"] = { fg = "#6aa2d7" },
    ["org.rust.MACRO"] = { fg = "#d5a563" },
    ["org.rust.METHOD"] = { fg = "#6aa2d7" },
    ["org.rust.METHOD_CALL"] = { fg = "#6aa2d7" },
    ["org.rust.MUT_BINDING"] = { effect = "underline", effect_color = "#bcbec4" },
    ["org.rust.MUT_SELF_PARAMETER"] = { effect = "underline", effect_color = "#bcbec4", fg = "#e59eae" },
    ["org.rust.Q_OPERATOR"] = { bold = true, fg = "#d8a460" },
    ["org.rust.SELF_EXPRESSION"] = { fg = "#e59eae" },
    ["org.rust.SELF_PARAMETER"] = { fg = "#e59eae" },
    ["org.rust.STATIC"] = { fg = "#c77dbb" },
    ["org.rust.STRUCT"] = { fg = "#a6bb77" },
    ["org.rust.TRAIT_METHOD"] = { fg = "#6aa2d7", italic = true },
    ["org.rust.TRAIT_METHOD_CALL"] = { fg = "#6aa2d7", italic = true },
    ["org.rust.TYPE_ALIAS"] = { fg = "#a6bb77" },
    ["org.rust.TYPE_PARAMETER"] = { fg = "#3cacac" },
    ["org.rust.UNION"] = { fg = "#a6bb77" },
    ["org.rust.UNSAFE_CODE"] = { bg = "#4e2c28" },'''),
    ...para('--', r'The last entries of the file, alphabetically sorted so that the org.rust.* group lands at the end, are the Rust plugin’s colours, and they are what makes this a Rust theme. Several things can be read straight off the table:'),
    blank,
    ...pt('--', r'one hue for types', r'STRUCT, TYPE_ALIAS, UNION and ENUM all use #a6bb77, a soft green. ENUM adds italic, and ENUM_VARIANT is a pale cyan italic. There is no TRAIT or LIFETIME entry, and init.lua says so where it falls back.'),
    ...pt('--', r'one hue for callables', r'FUNCTION, FUNCTION_CALL, METHOD, METHOD_CALL and the ASSOC_* entries are all #6aa2d7. Only trait methods differ, by italic.'),
    ...pt('--', r'self is pink', r'SELF_PARAMETER and SELF_EXPRESSION are #e59eae; the mutable self parameter adds an underline in the text colour.'),
    ...pt('--', r'mutability is an underline', r'MUT_BINDING has no fg at all, only an underline effect in the foreground grey. That is why init.lua turns it into an underline modifier and never a colour.'),
    ...pt('--', r'macros and the ? operator are gold', r'MACRO #d5a563, and Q_OPERATOR #d8a460 in bold, nearly the same hue, so error propagation looks related to macro calls.'),
    ...pt('--', r'unsafe is a background', r'UNSAFE_CODE has only a bg, #4e2c28. This is the entry whose use init.lua refuses (a code-wide band), replacing it with an underline from a separate plugin file.'),
    blank,
    ...sec(r'an attribute with every field'),
    ...para('--', r'Not every entry is a foreground. The effect field carries JetBrains’ decorations, translated by the converter into one of underline, undercurl, underdotted or strikethrough (its EFFECTS table maps the IntelliJ effect types 1 and 4 to underline, 2 to undercurl, 3 to strikethrough and 5 to underdotted; the spec asserts each mapping; type 0, a box, has no Neovim equivalent and is dropped). The stripe field is the colour of the marker on the editor’s right-hand error stripe, which init.lua reuses for coverage and satellite scrollbar signs. For example ERRORS_ATTRIBUTES has an undercurl in #fa6675 and a stripe #d64d5b, and the diagnostics underlines read the former while the scrollbar marks read the latter.'),
    ...sec(r'how the generator resolves values'),
    ...para('--', r'The docstring of tools/icls2nvim.py is the spec for this file. An attribute is resolved in a fixed order: its own explicit values first, then its baseAttributes chain (the converter is cycle safe), then the IDE’s fallback key for an empty entry. Entries that remain empty are dropped. The docstring also states the one limitation that matters: the scheme’s parent, Darcula, is not available to the converter, so values that only Darcula defines are simply missing. That is why init.lua defends with A[key] or {} and with the closest-colour fallbacks: a gap in the palette is expected, not exceptional.'),
    ...para('--', r'The converter uses only the Python standard library, and the spec runs it with -I -S, isolated mode and no site packages, to prove that. It also has a round trip test: it rebuilds a synthetic .icls from lua/island/palette.lua, converts that back, and expects the same colours and attributes. The round trip became the only way to keep the converter tested once the real export was removed from the repository.'),
    ...sec(r'trade-offs'),
    ...pt('--', r'generated data in git', r'the palette is committed rather than built on the user’s machine, so the config works without Python or the export, and the diff of a regeneration is readable. The price is that the source of truth is now the generated file; the header’s DO NOT EDIT warning is therefore load-bearing.'),
    ...pt('--', r'no override layer here', r'the file contains only what the scheme says. Every deviation (lifted comment colour, darker cursorline) is in init.lua, so a regeneration cannot overwrite a deliberate change, and you can see in one place what differs from the IDE.'),
    ...pt('--', r'sorted, flat keys', r'the keys are in alphabetical order and flat, which keeps the diff between two regenerations small and makes any name greppable.'),
    ...sec(r'tests'),
    ...para('--', r'tests/theme-island_spec.lua, in the section named palette.lua, reads the first 200 characters and requires the words GENERATED and DO NOT EDIT, checks the name "Dark", counts 63 colours and 173 attributes, and pins the three derived values. A palette that gains or loses an entry on regeneration therefore fails a test, and the author has to update the counts consciously.'),
    ...sec(r'history'),
    ...pt('--', r'cf842d0 (2026-10-12)', r'"Theme: Island Dark from RustRover scheme, permanent". The file arrives with 251 lines, alongside init.lua, the lualine theme, the converter and the Dark.icls export.'),
    ...pt('--', r'0da94f4 (2026-10-22)', r'"Remove tools/Dark.icls, keep only the generated palette". One header line changes; the data does not. It is the only edit the file has seen since creation.'),
  ],
);
